<center><TABLE width=100% cellpadding=0 summary="Title">
<TR width=70%><TD ALIGN="LEFT"><font size=6>
<b>How Do Transistors Work?</b></font></td>
<TD width=30% ALIGN="RIGHT"><b>(c)1995  William J. Beaty</b></td></TR>
<tr><td align="left"><br>
</td></tr></TABLE></center> 


<table width=100%><tr>
<td><font size=4><b>NO, HOW DO THEY <i>REALLY</i> WORK?</b></font></td>
<td align=right><a href="/amateur/trshort.html">Short vers.</a></td>
</tr></table>


<br><p>

When I first became interested in electronics as a kid, I sat down and
figured out how bipolar transistors work.  <br><p> Well, sort of.  <br><p>

I read many articles which explained the "Common Base" setup.  This was
the one used by the original inventors of the transistor.  In these
explanations, the Base is grounded and the input signal is applied to the
Emitter. Since the common-base amplifier is rarely used, I ended up having
to dream up my own explanation.  I based it on the little bits I already
knew about the Common Emitter configuration; the one where the Base is the
input, and where the output is taken from between the Collector and a
resistor.  It sort of worked, but I wasn't satisfied.  I was full of
niggling doubts.<br><p>

When I went into engineering school, I found it extremely odd that there
STILL were no good explanations of bipolar transistors.  Sure, there were
detailed mathematical treatments which let you design circuits or
investigate detailed SPICE models.  But not one of these told me HOW a
transistor works.  And mysteriously, nobody else seemed curious.  
Everyone else in school seemed to think that memorizing the equations was
the same as gaining understanding of the device.  (R. Feynman calls this
math-worship by the name "Greek viewpoint", as opposed to the "Babylonian
viewpoint" where the concepts are far more important than the equations.)  
I'm a total Babylonian.  For me, math is useless at the start.  Only after
I've attained a visual and gut-level understanding of a topic, then later
the math is a wonderful tool for refining it and adding all the details.  
However, math alone is not a genuine explanation.  Equations are recipes,
and they do not confer understanding (to say nothing about teaching expert
knowledge.)  Beware, it doesn't matter how high your engineering grade
point average might be, if all you know about transistors is the math,
then your employers can replace you with a student intern who knows how to
use a SPICE application.<br><p>

Now many years have passed and I think I see the problem...

<blockquote>
Traditional transistor explanations basically *suck.*  
</blockquote>

The ones I see in textbooks and hobby magazines are terrible.  They're
full of errors and contradictions.  Below are my ideas on how transistors
REALLY work.  As you'll soon see, several new concepts are required.  
Also, this is a discussion of transistor internals.  If all you want is to
design circuits, it might be easier for you to just memorize the equations
rather than to imagine what really happens inside.  But if you DO manage
to decode my explanations and crude ASCII artwork, I think you'll be in
the elite minority who <i>really understands</i> transistors.  I've found
that even most working engineers have no good mental picture of bipolar
transistor operation, so if you attain an understanding of transistors,
you'll surpass many of the experts.<br><p>

<hr>


First of all, you must abandon the idea that CURRENT travels in
transistors or flows inside of wires.  Yes, you heard me right.  
<i>Current does not flow in circuits.</i>  Electric current <i>never</i> 
flows, since electric current is a flow of something else.  (Ask yourself 
this:  
what's the stuff that flows
in a river, is it "current" or is it called "water?")<br><p>

So what flows 
inside of wires?

The stuff that moves within wires is not named Electric Current.  Intead
it is called Electric Charge.  It's the charge that flows, never the
current.  And in rivers or plumbing, it's the water that flows along, not
the "current."  <br><p>

<b>Second:</b> the charge does not push itself along, but instead it's
pushed by potential difference; it's pushed by the voltage-fields inside
the conductive material.  Charges are not squirted out of power supplies
as if the power supply was some sort of cannon.  If you imagine that
charges come out of the positive or negative end of the power supply and
then spread throughout the circuit, you've made a fundamental mistake.  
Wires do not act like empty water pipes, and power supplies do not supply
any electrons.  To create a flow, a power supply DOES NOT inject any
charges into the wires.<b><p>

<b>Third:</b> have you discovered the big 'secret' of visualizing electric
circuits?  <blockquote>ALL CONDUCTORS ARE ALREADY FULL OF CHARGE
</blockquote> Both the wires and the silicon behave like pre-filled water
pipes.  A block of conductor is like a water tank.  Metals contain a vast
quantity of movable electrons which forms a sort of "electric fluid"
within the metal.  Physicists call this the "electron sea" of metals.  
This idea is very necessary when visualizing transistors.  Semiconductors
are always full of movable charge-stuff, even when a transistor is sitting
on the shelf disconnected from everything.  When a voltage is applied
across a piece of silicon, the charges within the material are driven into
motion. <br><p>

To understand circuitry, don't trace out the circuit path starting 
at the
terminals of the power supply.
Instead look at any component on the schematic.  If there is a voltage
applied across that component, then the charges within that component will 
start to
flow.  Lets modify the old "flashlight explanation" which we all were 
taught in grade school.  Here is the corrected version: <br><p>

<blockquote>Wires are full of movable electric charges (all conductors 
are.)   If you connect some wires into a circle, you form an "electric 
circuit" which contains a movable conveyor belt made of charges.  We cut 
this circle we insert a battery and a light bulb.  The battery acts as a 
charge pump, while the light bulb offers friction.  The charges flow, and 
the bulb lights up.  Let's follow them.<br><p>

The charges start out inside the light bulb filament.  (No, not inside the
battery.  We start at the <i>bulb</i>.)  They're forced to flow along 
through the filament.  Then they flow out into the first wire and move 
along to the
battery.  The battery pumps the charges through itself and back out.  The
charges leave the other battery terminal, then they flow through the
second wire and wind up back inside the light bulb filament.  The battery
acts as a pump.  The wires act like a fluid-filled conveyor belt.  
The light bulb acts like "friction;" getting hot when its own natural
charges are forced to flow along.  </blockquote> <br><p> <hr> <br><p>

Brief review:<br>
<blockquote>
1. THE STUFF THAT FLOWS THROUGH CONDUCTORS IS CALLED CHARGE. 
 ("CURRENT" DOESN'T FLOW.)<br><p>
2. THE CHARGE INSIDE CONDUCTORS IS SWEPT ALONG BY VOLTAGE FIELDS.<br><p>
3. ALL WIRES ARE "PRE-FILLED" WITH A VAST AMOUNT OF MOVABLE CHARGE<br><p>
4. BATTERIES AND POWER SUPPLIES ARE CHARGE-PUMPS.<br><p>
5. LIGHT BULBS AND RESISTORS ACT "FRICTIONALLY."<br>
</blockquote>


One last thing:  The difference between a conductor and an insulator is
simple:  a conductor is like a pre-filled water pipe, while an insulator
is like a pipe that's choked with ice.  Both contain the "electric stuff;" 
they're 
both full of electrically charged particles.  But the 
"stuff" inside an insulator can't move.  Apply a pressure-difference 
across a
water pipe, and the water flows.  But with an empty pipe, there's nothing 
there, so the flow does not occur.  And with an ice-choked pipe, the stuff 
is trapped and doesn't budge.  (In other words, voltage causes 
charge-flow in conductors, but it can't cause charge-flow in 
insulators because the charges are immobilized.)
<br><p>

<hr>
<p>

One last thing before diving into transistors.  SILICON IS VERY DIFFERENT 
THAN
METAL.  Metals are full of movable charges... but so is doped silicon.  
How are they different?
Yes, there's that matter of "band gap" and the electrons versus holes, but 
that's not the
important thing.  The important thing is quite simple:  metals have vast
quantities of charge, but silicon does not.  For example in copper, every
single copper atom donates one movable electron to the "sea of charge."  
But in
doped silicon, only one atom in a billion donates a movable charge.  In
silicon, you can sweep the charges out of the material by using a few 
volts of potential,
while in a metal it would take billions of volts to accomplish the same
thing.  Or in other words:

<blockquote>
6.  THE CHARGE INSIDE OF SEMICONDUCTORS IS LIKE A COMPRESSIBLE GAS, WHILE 
THE CHARGE INSIDE OF METALS IS LIKE A DENSE INCOMPRESSIBLE LIQUID.

</blockquote>
  Sweeping away the charges is the same as converting a material 
from conductor to insulator.  If silicon is like a rubber hose, then it's 
a hose which contains compressible gas, and you can easily squeeze it shut 
and stop the flow.  But if copper is also like a rubber hose, then instead 
it's like a 
hose full of iron slugs.  You can squeeze and squeeze, but you can't smash 
them out of the way.  But with air hoses and silicon blocks, you can pinch 
the pathway shut and stop the flow.

<br><p>
<hr>
<br><p>

OK, let's look at the way that transistors are usually explained. 
<br><p>

To turn on an NPN transistor, a voltage is applied
across the base and emitter terminals.  This causes electrons in the Base
wire to move away from the transistor itself and flow out towards
the
power supply.  This in turn pulls electrons out of the P-type base region, 
leaving
'holes' behind, and the 'holes' act like positive charges which are pushed
in the opposite direction from the direction of electron current.  What
SEEMS to happen is that the base wire injects positive charges into the
base region.  It spews holes.  It injects charge.<br><p>

(Note that I'm describing CHARGE FLOW here, not
positive-charge "conventional current.") 
<br><p>

<pre>               |
         ______|______
        |             |
        | COLLECTOR N |
        |_____________|                       ELECTRONS ARE PULLED FROM THE
        |             |  ----->               BASE REGION AND INTO THE WIRE,
        | BASE      P |______________         WHICH CREATES POSITIVE "HOLES"
        |_____________|              |  +     WHICH SPEW OUT INTO THE BASE
        |             |          ____|____    REGION.
        | EMITTER  N  |            _____
        |_____________|          _________
               |                   _____
               |_____________________|  -

</pre>

That's part of the conventional explanation.  Why is all of this important
to transistor operation?  ***IT'S NOT!***  The base current is not 
important to transistor operation.   It's just a byproduct of the
REAL operation, which involves an insulating layer called the Depletion
Region.  See the diagram below.<br><p>

The Depletion Region is an insulating layer existing between the base
region and the emitter region.  Why is it there?  It exists because the 
Base region
is p-type silicon, it exists because p-type silicon is full of naturally-
occurring movable holes, and because the p-type silicon is touching n-type
silicon.  P-type silicon is like the beads of an abacus, and the holes are 
like gaps in the rows of beads.  Move one bead, and a hole has moved the 
other way.  Touch the p-type silicon to the n-type, and wandering 
electrons fall into the holes.  
Holes in the p-type region flow together and many are cancelled
by the movable electrons that exist in the N-type emitter, and this leaves
a thin layer between N and P sections which lacks movable charges.<br><p>

Remember: a conductor is NOT a substance which allows charges to pass.
Don't forget #3 above.  A conductor is actually any substance which
contains charges which are MOVABLE.  Anything that lacks movable charges
is an insulator.  Inside the depletion layer, all the opposite charges
have fallen together and vanished.  The gaps in the abacus beads are gone,
so no beads can move anymore.  Lacking mobile charges, the silicon has
turned into an insulator.  When there's no voltage applied across the
base/emitter terminals, this insulating layer is fairly thick, and the
transistor acts like a switch which has been turned off. <br><p>

I like to visualize that normally a transistor's silicon is a 
shiny
silver conductor (sort of like metal) except for this insulating layer
between the P and N regions which acts more like a layer of insulating 
glass.  <br><p>

<pre>               |
         ______|______
        |             |
        | COLLECTOR N |
        |_____________|                       
        |             |                
        | BASE      P |--
        |_____________|           
         _____________    <-- Glasslike "depletion layer"        
        |             |          
        | EMITTER  N  | 
        |_____________|      
               |             
               |
</pre>

When voltage is applied between base and emitter, this insulating layer
<i>changes thickness.	</i>  If (+)voltage is applied to the 
p-type, to the base wire,
while a (-) polairty is applied to the n-type, to the emitter wire, then
electrons in the n-type are pushed towards the holes in the p-type.
The insulating layer becomes so thin that the clouds of electrons and
holes start meeting and combining.  A current therefore exists in the
base/emitter circuit.  But this current is not important to transistor
action.  The important thing to notice is that the *VOLTAGE* across the
base/emitter has caused the insulating Depletion Layer to become so thin
that the charges can now flow across it.  It's as if the transistor 
contains a
layer of glass whose thickness can be varied by varying a voltage.  The
layer becomes thinner when base/emitter voltage is increased.  This
happens because the voltage pushes the holes and the electrons towards
each other, reducing the size of the empty insulating region between the
masses of holes and electrons, and allowing the stragglers to jump across
the insulator.  The depletion layer is a voltage-controlled switch which
"closes" when the right polarity of voltage is applied.  It is also a
proportional switch, since a small voltage can close it only partially.  
For silicon material, charges start jumping across when the voltage is 
around
0.3V.  Raise the voltage to 0.7V and the current gets very high.  (That's
for silicon.  Other materials have different turn-on voltages.)  The
larger the voltage, the thinner the insulating layer, so the higher the
current in the entire transistor. By applying the right voltage, you can
thicken or thin the depletion layer as desired, creating an open,
closed, or partially open switch. <br><p>

See what's happening here?  THE TRANSISTOR IS NOT CONTROLLED BY CURRENT.
Instead it is controlled by the base/emitter voltage.


<blockquote>
7. THE P-TYPE AND N-TYPE ARE CONDUCTORS BECAUSE THEY CONTAIN MOVABLE 
CHARGES.
<br><p>
8. A LAYER OF INSULATING MATERIAL APPEARS WHERE P-TYPE AND N-TYPE TOUCH.
<br><p>
9. THE INSULATING LAYER CAN BE MADE THIN BY APPLYING A VOLTAGE.
</blockquote>


<pre>
               |
         ______|______
        |             |
        | COLLECTOR N |
        |_____________|
        |             |  ---->
        | BASE      P |______________         
        |=============|              |  +     With a small voltage applied,
        |             |          ____|____    the depletion layer gets thin,
        | EMITTER  N  |            _____      charges start crossing it,
        |_____________|          _________    and a small current appears.
               |                   _____      The "switch" is only partly 
               |_____________________|  -     closed!
                          <----- </pre> 

<br><p>
<font size=6> OK, on to <a href="/amateur/transis2.html">PART TWO</a></font>
&nbsp&nbsp&nbsp&nbsp&nbsp&nbsp
